WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.840 --> 00:00:10.720
If you don't know anything about medieval&nbsp;
art, you might reasonably assume old&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.720 --> 00:00:16.400
illustrated texts featured basic depictions of&nbsp;
historical and religious figures. But medieval&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.400 --> 00:00:22.880
art is overflowing with absurdity: particularly&nbsp;
when it comes to depictions of medieval beasts.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.400 --> 00:00:29.920
Beyond the dragons and unicorns you might&nbsp;
be familiar with is a truly insane world of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.920 --> 00:00:34.560
lesser-known monsters which stretched the&nbsp;
imagination with their utter bizarreness.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.760 --> 00:00:40.480
So today we're going to take a look at&nbsp;
the top 10 strangest of these creatures:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.480 --> 00:00:44.480
bearing in mind it was exceedingly&nbsp;
difficult to cut this down to just 10.

00:00:47.040 --> 00:00:54.480
Number 10: monopod. Mythological creatures with&nbsp;
a single leg ending in a massive foot, monopod's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.480 --> 00:01:00.080
name comes from a Greek term aptly meaning&nbsp;
"one-foot." Medieval artists often drew them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.080 --> 00:01:05.600
lying down, with their foot stretched over their&nbsp;
head. This comes from a legend that the monopods&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.600 --> 00:01:10.640
have a habit of protecting themselves from the sun&nbsp;
using the shade cast by their foot, sort of like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.640 --> 00:01:18.640
a natural umbrella. Number nine: bonnacon Though&nbsp;
at first glance these mythic creatures resemble&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.640 --> 00:01:25.200
ordinary bulls, the bonnacon was said to eject&nbsp;
large amounts of caustic excrement at hunters.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.200 --> 00:01:29.360
While medieval texts often communicated&nbsp;
moral lessons through fictitious beasts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.360 --> 00:01:34.960
the banaycon seems to have no symbolic meaning&nbsp;
whatsoever. If anything, it only shows lowbrow&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.960 --> 00:01:42.400
humor hasn't changed much over the centuries.&nbsp;
Number eight: sea monk Ducking just slightly out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.400 --> 00:01:47.600
of the medieval period for a creature I had to&nbsp;
include, the sea monk is an organism allegedly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.600 --> 00:01:53.440
captured off the Danish coast which outwardly&nbsp;
resembled a monk in a habit. Supposedly, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.440 --> 00:01:59.360
account claimed the animal's resemblance to a monk&nbsp;
was just a coincidence. The creature had the inner&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.360 --> 00:02:04.560
workings of an ordinary fish, and just happened&nbsp;
to superficially resemble a monk on the outside.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.320 --> 00:02:12.720
Number 7: salamander This next one might seem like&nbsp;
an odd inclusion, since the amphibious salamander&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.720 --> 00:02:18.560
isn't mythical at all. But medieval legends of&nbsp;
salamanders were, let's say, a little off from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.560 --> 00:02:23.760
the real-life organism. Alchemists believes&nbsp;
salamanders were fireproof, which is strange&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.760 --> 00:02:29.280
enough, but many depictions of salamanders deviate&nbsp;
wildly from their actual appearance. Why so many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.280 --> 00:02:35.840
medieval artists assigned hair and human faces to&nbsp;
salamanders is, unfortunately, largely unclear.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.200 --> 00:02:43.600
Number 6: amphisbien Getting its name from&nbsp;
the greek term amphis, meaning "both ways,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.600 --> 00:02:49.280
and bien, meaning "to go" this rather amusing&nbsp;
monster was a serpent with two opposing heads.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.280 --> 00:02:53.520
According to legend the unfortunate&nbsp;
reptile had difficulty moving around&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.520 --> 00:02:56.240
due to the dual minds tugging&nbsp;
it in different directions.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.240 --> 00:03:05.120
Number 5 Shurale The only creature on this list&nbsp;
originating in Russia, Shurale is a long-fingered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.120 --> 00:03:10.240
humanoid from folk tales who dwells in the woods.&nbsp;
Though not too far off from trolls or goblins,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.240 --> 00:03:15.920
what makes Shurale so unusual is its behavior&nbsp;
according to legends, shirali lures the unlucky&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.920 --> 00:03:22.640
into thickets where he... tickles them to death.&nbsp;
Something about that is genuinely unsettling.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.920 --> 00:03:31.040
Number four: snails One of my personal favorites:&nbsp;
like the salamander, medieval snails are based on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.040 --> 00:03:35.360
their real-life counterparts, but deviate&nbsp;
wildly from what you'd find in nature.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.400 --> 00:03:41.440
Manuscripts portray snails as enormous&nbsp;
weapon-wielding juggernauts, menacing knights&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.440 --> 00:03:48.080
and helpless villagers. Hilariously, historians&nbsp;
aren't entirely sure why so many margins of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.080 --> 00:03:53.920
medieval texts feature these super-sized mollusks:&nbsp;
with theories ranging from political propaganda,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.920 --> 00:04:00.320
to the snails representing the slow but&nbsp;
inevitable approach of death which awaits us all.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.320 --> 00:04:05.600
Number three: Blemmyes. Headless creatures&nbsp;
with faces on their chests, the intelligent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.600 --> 00:04:10.480
and communal Blemmyes are another one of my&nbsp;
favorites. More so than the other entries&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.480 --> 00:04:15.440
on this list, medieval European scholars believe&nbsp;
the Blemmyes to be more than the stuff of fables.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.440 --> 00:04:20.400
Many believed the Blemmyes were a real species&nbsp;
of humanoid living in other parts of the world,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.400 --> 00:04:24.320
to the point where they're featured on&nbsp;
several maps... just sort of hanging out.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.320 --> 00:04:32.720
Number two: the vegetable lamb of Tartary We're&nbsp;
really in the strange stuff now. The vegetable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.720 --> 00:04:37.520
lamb is a legendary plant which sprouted&nbsp;
fruit identical in appearance to sheep.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.520 --> 00:04:44.400
The sheep even contained blood and bones, despite&nbsp;
being plants. Holy Roman Empire historian Sigmund&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.400 --> 00:04:49.760
Von Herbertstien even went as far as to say he'd&nbsp;
heard from too many credible sources to doubt&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.760 --> 00:04:54.560
the plant's existence. Although Herbertstein was&nbsp;
obviously mistaken, modern scholars actually have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.560 --> 00:05:01.040
a theory that the legends origin stems from widely&nbsp;
circulated misinterpretations of cotton plants.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.040 --> 00:05:08.640
Pretty interesting stuff. Number one: Gryllus&nbsp;
I simply couldn't resist putting this little&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.640 --> 00:05:14.080
guy at the very top. What you see is what you get&nbsp;
with this one: a pair of legs topped with a head,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.080 --> 00:05:18.080
parading in packs around the margins of&nbsp;
medieval manuscripts. They're simple,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.080 --> 00:05:23.520
but there's something so fundamentally absurd&nbsp;
about the Gryllus: especially considering their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.520 --> 00:05:29.520
origin is almost completely unknown. What&nbsp;
an incredible, albeit inexplicable creation.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.600 --> 00:05:35.920
And that's where our list comes to an end. I hope&nbsp;
you had fun going on this journey through the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.920 --> 00:05:40.960
bizarre world of medieval monsters with me. Please&nbsp;
consider subscribing, and leaving this video a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.960 --> 00:05:51.840
like if you enjoyed the content. Thank you so much&nbsp;
for watching, and I'll see you in the next video.

